Yes, that works and keeps the relay small:
- the login page takes the face name; the relay mails a short code to that member stored email and answers with a form for the code;
- the member row holds only a hash of the code and its expiry (about 10 minutes, one use);
- a right code opens a session: a random cookie kept in the relay memory, never on disk, so a relay restart just means logging in again.
The one new need: the relay must send mail, through an SMTP account or a mail API whose key sits in this goal store, the relay owner one.

Not green yet; fixed in the box (v6):
- the old Postmark line is gone, Mailgun with the EU URL https://api.eu.mailgun.net stands;
- a sandbox domain only mails addresses you authorized in Mailgun, so members need a verified domain of yours there;
- the cap on codes is in.
One real gap: the relay sends the mail, so the key must sit on the relay box, and the goal secrets store is not built. My proposal: a file relay-state/mail.json on spirit-3 (URL, domain, key) that you place by hand, never in git.
Is that file where the relay reads the key?

can't figure out how to find anything on that site, it says: send a production email like this:

import FormData from "form-data"; // form-data v4.0.1
import Mailgun from "mailgun.js"; // mailgun.js v11.1.0

async function sendSimpleMessage() {
  const mailgun = new Mailgun(FormData);
  const mg = mailgun.client({
    username: "api",
    key: process.env.API_KEY || "API_KEY",
    // When you have an EU-domain, you must specify the endpoint:
    // url: "https://api.eu.mailgun.net"
  });
  try {
    const data = await mg.messages.create("spirit.andyflinn.com", {
      from: "Mailgun Sandbox <postmaster@spirit.andyflinn.com>",
      to: ["ANDREAS THUT <countinn@gmail.com>"],
      subject: "Hello ANDREAS THUT",
      text: "Congratulations ANDREAS THUT, you just sent an email with Mailgun! You are truly awesome!",
    });

    console.log(data); // logs response data
  } catch (error) {
    console.log(error); //logs any error
  }
}

In the box as your flow. Yes, I reused the login cycle for onboarding; your one-field page does that cleanly.
What I would build with it, as statements:
- the page remembers which email a code was sent to with a short cookie, since the user then types only the code;
- the long session is kept with the member as a hash and an expiry, so a relay restart does not log people out;
- Natter takes the pasted email:token where a relay invite is entered today.
One thing must be decided first: at claim, the invite label becomes the member public label, so an email label would be published in the relay roll. What should a member public label be when the invite is an email?

The same three fields, plus driver:
{ "driver": "mailgun", "url": "https://api.mailgun.net", "domain": "spirit.andyflinn.com", "key": "<your key, unchanged>" }
Only driver is new; keep your key as it is in the file and never in chat.

