Before the red, three shapes the box leaves open; one at a time, the other two named so you see them coming:
- (now) how a peer call reaches a node family like fs.stat;
- (next) whether the app server selector lists only apps or every family now;
- (last) where the registering that leaves introSpector lands: verbTable or server.js.
The first. Today the node's own verbs are web handlers on its loopback door. Plainest: apiDoor, after the grant check, posts the call to the node's own loopback door, so it runs exactly as a loopback call does, as the owner (which is what a grant of that verb means). The other way, every node handler learning to take a caller, touches every module.
My lean: the loopback post. Agreed?

In the box (v14): apps get process/js/<app>/family/ for their AGENTS.md and verb files, apart from their code; FS is every family's owner verb over its folder; warnings are read through FS, not AGENTS.<verb>.

FS is a new verb on every family, so its shape needs your yes. Proposed, one verb, one op per call:
- FS {op: "list"} answers the folder's file names;
- FS {op: "load", path} answers {text};
- FS {op: "save", path, text} writes it;
- FS {op: "delete", path} removes it.
A path is a file name in the folder; anything with a slash or .. is refused.

Yes to that shape?

